1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,760
Every machine humanity has ever
landed on another world

2
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:08,880
sits inside a bubble of light
less than two light-hours across—

3
00:00:09,540 --> 00:00:15,120
while the nearest star beyond our Sun
is 4.24 light-years away.

4
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:20,800
Inside that small neighbourhood, we have
set cameras down on a scorching twin

5
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:23,900
where lead melts on the ground,

6
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:29,260
and on a frozen red desert where nuclear
rovers drive for years through the dust.

7
00:00:30,480 --> 00:00:35,760
This is one continuous journey outward
from the ground moving under your feet—

8
00:00:35,760 --> 00:00:38,580
past the worlds our landers have touched,

9
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:41,980
through the comet cloud
at the edge of the Sun's reach,

10
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:46,880
and out to the giant stars
that forged the atoms in your hands.

11
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:49,840
It begins right where you are sitting.

12
00:00:52,822 --> 00:00:54,902
Right now, you are moving.

13
00:00:55,302 --> 00:00:58,402
If you are in Britain, the
ground under you is carrying

14
00:00:58,402 --> 00:01:01,882
you east at about a thousand
kilometres an hour.

15
00:01:02,422 --> 00:01:05,902
At the equator it is faster
than a jet airliner.

16
00:01:06,462 --> 00:01:09,082
And you feel nothing at all.

17
00:01:10,022 --> 00:01:12,682
So what would happen if
Earth stopped spinning?

18
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:14,342
Not slowly.

19
00:01:14,402 --> 00:01:15,802
All at once.

20
00:01:15,922 --> 00:01:17,542
Now imagine the impossible.

21
00:01:17,742 --> 00:01:20,702
Earth's rock stops turning in an instant.

22
00:01:20,902 --> 00:01:22,442
But the air does not.

23
00:01:22,882 --> 00:01:24,682
Nothing has told it to stop.

24
00:01:25,062 --> 00:01:27,762
At the equator the atmosphere
would keep going east at

25
00:01:27,762 --> 00:01:30,742
more than one and a half
thousand kilometres an hour.

26
00:01:31,022 --> 00:01:33,182
That is faster than the speed of sound,

27
00:01:33,182 --> 00:01:37,242
and several times faster than the
strongest gust of wind ever measured.

28
00:01:37,702 --> 00:01:42,502
The oceans would keep moving too, and
rise up against every eastern coast.

29
00:01:42,862 --> 00:01:44,042
Why east?

30
00:01:44,042 --> 00:01:46,922
Because that is the way we
were all already going.

31
00:01:47,122 --> 00:01:50,522
Anything not fixed to the ground
would carry on east as well,

32
00:01:50,562 --> 00:01:53,702
just as you lurch forwards
when a bus brakes hard.

33
00:01:54,282 --> 00:01:58,682
Here in Britain the wind would arrive
at about a thousand kilometres an hour.

34
00:01:59,242 --> 00:02:01,162
But here is a strange detail.

35
00:02:01,542 --> 00:02:05,462
Stand exactly at the North Pole,
and you would hardly notice.

36
00:02:05,542 --> 00:02:07,822
You were barely moving in the first place.

37
00:02:07,902 --> 00:02:10,722
You were just turning on
the spot, once a day.

38
00:02:11,162 --> 00:02:12,602
And it cannot happen.

39
00:02:12,642 --> 00:02:14,162
There is no brake.

40
00:02:14,362 --> 00:02:17,782
The energy stored in Earth's
spin is as much as all the

41
00:02:17,782 --> 00:02:21,802
sunlight that reaches Earth in
about forty thousand years.

42
00:02:22,182 --> 00:02:25,862
Nothing in our solar system
could take that away in a moment.

43
00:02:26,162 --> 00:02:27,602
Why don't you feel it?

44
00:02:27,602 --> 00:02:29,382
Because the speed is steady.

45
00:02:29,502 --> 00:02:33,482
Sit in a smooth airliner and you can
pour a drink without spilling a drop.

46
00:02:33,582 --> 00:02:36,902
Nothing pushes you, because
nothing is changing.

47
00:02:37,102 --> 00:02:41,462
Your body only notices a change
in speed, never the speed itself.

48
00:02:41,622 --> 00:02:44,642
You can still see the
spin, every single day.

49
00:02:44,642 --> 00:02:46,382
The Sun does not rise.

50
00:02:46,382 --> 00:02:48,882
Your side of Earth turns towards it.

51
00:02:49,102 --> 00:02:55,242
Earth turns from west to east, and that is
why the Sun always comes up in the east,

52
00:02:55,242 --> 00:02:58,382
and why the stars wheel
across the sky at night.

53
00:02:58,762 --> 00:03:00,742
The spin does leave clues.

54
00:03:00,882 --> 00:03:04,202
Because the ground at the
equator swings round so fast,

55
00:03:04,202 --> 00:03:08,242
you weigh about half of one percent
less there than at the poles.

56
00:03:08,342 --> 00:03:10,462
And the whole planet bulges.

57
00:03:10,662 --> 00:03:13,122
Earth is about forty-three
kilometres wider

58
00:03:13,122 --> 00:03:16,422
across the equator than
it is from pole to pole.

59
00:03:16,642 --> 00:03:21,202
In 1851, in Paris, Léon
Foucault hung a heavy ball

60
00:03:21,202 --> 00:03:25,402
from a wire sixty-seven metres
long and set it swinging.

61
00:03:25,622 --> 00:03:29,462
Over the hours, the line of
its swing slowly turned.

62
00:03:29,582 --> 00:03:31,462
The pendulum was not turning.

63
00:03:31,462 --> 00:03:32,862
The floor was.

64
00:03:32,962 --> 00:03:37,262
Anyone could stand there and watch
Earth spin without looking at the sky.

65
00:03:37,562 --> 00:03:39,702
We make one of these every week.

66
00:03:39,782 --> 00:03:42,482
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

67
00:03:42,982 --> 00:03:45,782
So a sudden stop is a thought experiment.

68
00:03:46,002 --> 00:03:47,562
What about a slow one?

69
00:03:47,982 --> 00:03:50,882
Suppose Earth slowed down gently,

70
00:03:50,882 --> 00:03:54,682
over thousands of years,
until it did not turn at all.

71
00:03:55,462 --> 00:03:57,702
Remember that bulge at the equator?

72
00:03:58,222 --> 00:04:00,942
The spin is what holds
the oceans out there.

73
00:04:01,342 --> 00:04:05,102
Without it, the water would
drain away towards the poles.

74
00:04:05,662 --> 00:04:07,322
Mapmakers have modelled it.

75
00:04:07,662 --> 00:04:11,142
The seas gather into
two great polar oceans,

76
00:04:11,222 --> 00:04:16,262
and a single enormous band of dry land
wraps around the middle of the world.

77
00:04:17,102 --> 00:04:19,102
And the day itself would change.

78
00:04:19,342 --> 00:04:24,962
With no spin at all, the Sun would cross
the sky just once as Earth went round it.

79
00:04:25,522 --> 00:04:28,652
One day would last a whole year: six

80
00:04:28,652 --> 00:04:31,782
months of daylight, then
six months of night.

81
00:04:32,442 --> 00:04:35,922
The day side would bake
under a Sun that never sets,

82
00:04:36,022 --> 00:04:38,802
and the night side would
freeze in the dark,

83
00:04:38,822 --> 00:04:42,602
with the wind always pouring
from one side to the other.

84
00:04:43,902 --> 00:04:46,022
We have a neighbour that comes close.

85
00:04:46,222 --> 00:04:50,902
Venus turns once every two hundred
and forty-three Earth days,

86
00:04:51,062 --> 00:04:52,682
and it turns backwards.

87
00:04:53,062 --> 00:04:55,381
Its day is longer than its year.

88
00:04:56,122 --> 00:05:00,582
If you saw our Venus film, you know
how strange that world became.

89
00:05:01,042 --> 00:05:02,522
Here is the twist.

90
00:05:02,762 --> 00:05:05,702
Earth is slowing down already, right now.

91
00:05:06,142 --> 00:05:10,062
The Moon pulls on our oceans
and raises the tides.

92
00:05:10,242 --> 00:05:15,922
As Earth turns beneath those tides, the
water drags a little against the seabed,

93
00:05:15,922 --> 00:05:18,702
like a hand resting on a spinning wheel.

94
00:05:19,262 --> 00:05:22,742
Every day, a tiny amount of spin is lost.

95
00:05:23,122 --> 00:05:24,522
Where does it go?

96
00:05:24,682 --> 00:05:26,082
Into the Moon.

97
00:05:26,242 --> 00:05:28,882
The tides tug the Moon forwards,

98
00:05:28,922 --> 00:05:33,342
and it drifts about four centimetres
further from us every year.

99
00:05:34,022 --> 00:05:38,322
If you saw our Moon film, this
is the other half of that story.

100
00:05:38,702 --> 00:05:41,962
And the Moon shows us
where this road leads.

101
00:05:42,662 --> 00:05:46,442
Long ago, Earth's pull
slowed the Moon's own

102
00:05:46,442 --> 00:05:50,662
spin until one turn
took exactly one orbit.

103
00:05:51,022 --> 00:05:54,682
That is why the Moon always
shows us the same face.

104
00:05:54,922 --> 00:05:56,602
It did not stop spinning.

105
00:05:56,602 --> 00:05:58,002
It got locked.

106
00:05:58,422 --> 00:05:59,822
How do we know?

107
00:06:00,222 --> 00:06:01,882
Ancient eclipses.

108
00:06:02,182 --> 00:06:03,922
Astronomers in Babylon,

109
00:06:03,922 --> 00:06:09,542
China and medieval Europe wrote down
where and when the Sun went dark.

110
00:06:09,802 --> 00:06:12,502
If Earth had kept
spinning at today's rate,

111
00:06:12,542 --> 00:06:14,482
those eclipses would have been seen from

112
00:06:14,482 --> 00:06:18,082
places hundreds or thousands
of kilometres away.

113
00:06:18,522 --> 00:06:22,422
The records only fit if the
day has been growing longer,

114
00:06:22,722 --> 00:06:26,762
by nearly two thousandths
of a second every century.

115
00:06:27,302 --> 00:06:28,822
That sounds like nothing.

116
00:06:29,022 --> 00:06:30,422
But give it time.

117
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:36,362
Corals lay down a fine growth line
every day, and a thicker band each year.

118
00:06:36,762 --> 00:06:39,922
Fossil corals about three
hundred and eighty million

119
00:06:39,922 --> 00:06:44,362
years old show about four
hundred daily lines in a year.

120
00:06:44,842 --> 00:06:48,962
Back then, a day was only
about twenty-two hours long.

121
00:06:49,442 --> 00:06:52,302
So who notices two
thousandths of a second?

122
00:06:52,302 --> 00:06:53,702
Your phone does.

123
00:06:54,282 --> 00:06:58,142
Since the late 1960s the second
has been set by atomic clocks,

124
00:06:58,142 --> 00:07:00,562
which do not care how fast Earth turns.

125
00:07:00,562 --> 00:07:04,682
But the day, measured by the Sun,
does not quite fit the clocks.

126
00:07:04,762 --> 00:07:08,582
So since 1972, timekeepers have
added an extra second to the

127
00:07:08,582 --> 00:07:13,402
world's clocks twenty-seven times,
to keep noon where it belongs.

128
00:07:13,722 --> 00:07:16,522
The last one came at the end of 2016.

129
00:07:16,562 --> 00:07:18,282
And the day wobbles.

130
00:07:18,602 --> 00:07:24,162
Earth's molten core, its oceans and
even melting ice all nudge the spin.

131
00:07:24,402 --> 00:07:28,282
In the last few years the planet has
actually been running a touch fast,

132
00:07:28,282 --> 00:07:31,602
and some days have come in
more than a millisecond short.

133
00:07:32,022 --> 00:07:36,582
Timekeepers now plan to stop
adding leap seconds by 2035.

134
00:07:36,842 --> 00:07:39,002
So Earth will never stop spinning.

135
00:07:39,102 --> 00:07:42,102
Even the Moon's slow
brake would need tens of

136
00:07:42,102 --> 00:07:45,522
billions of years to lock
our day to its month,

137
00:07:45,562 --> 00:07:49,542
and long before then the Sun will
have changed beyond recognition.

138
00:07:49,722 --> 00:07:53,382
Your days will stay twenty-four
hours, to within a blink.

139
00:07:54,262 --> 00:07:58,402
What if the most surprising thing about
your day is not that it might end,

140
00:07:58,402 --> 00:08:00,702
but that it has always been changing?

141
00:08:00,942 --> 00:08:04,102
The bigger question is the
one underneath all of this.

142
00:08:04,442 --> 00:08:07,162
Something set Earth spinning
in the first place,

143
00:08:07,202 --> 00:08:09,902
about four and a half billion years ago.

144
00:08:10,362 --> 00:08:16,122
Every sunrise you have ever seen is
that first push, still running down.

145
00:08:16,212 --> 00:08:19,212
Earth's 24-hour spin and liquid oceans

146
00:08:19,212 --> 00:08:22,072
kept its carbon locked safely
in stone beneath the sea.

147
00:08:23,192 --> 00:08:26,812
Step 40 million kilometres inward
to our closest planetary neighbour,

148
00:08:27,112 --> 00:08:31,672
where a single day lasts
243 Earth days—

149
00:08:31,672 --> 00:08:35,452
and that same carbon
escaped into the sky.

150
00:08:38,222 --> 00:08:41,002
Venus is the planet most like Earth.

151
00:08:41,302 --> 00:08:42,882
So what happened to it?

152
00:08:43,582 --> 00:08:47,302
This is the ground on Venus, seen
through the clouds by radar.

153
00:08:47,662 --> 00:08:48,902
Why radar?

154
00:08:48,902 --> 00:08:51,342
Because ordinary light cannot get through.

155
00:08:51,542 --> 00:08:53,662
It is hot enough to melt lead.

156
00:08:53,982 --> 00:08:57,542
The air presses down like the
sea nearly a kilometre deep.

157
00:08:57,762 --> 00:09:02,062
No machine we have landed there could
survive it for much more than two hours.

158
00:09:02,682 --> 00:09:05,562
And this world is almost
the size of yours.

159
00:09:05,802 --> 00:09:07,542
It is made of the same rock.

160
00:09:07,662 --> 00:09:10,502
It even takes in less
sunlight than Earth does,

161
00:09:10,502 --> 00:09:13,782
and it is still the hottest
planet in the solar system.

162
00:09:13,982 --> 00:09:16,622
Start with what the two planets share.

163
00:09:17,222 --> 00:09:23,602
Venus is ninety-five percent as wide as
Earth, with about four-fifths of its mass.

164
00:09:24,022 --> 00:09:26,462
It formed from the same kind of rock,

165
00:09:26,462 --> 00:09:30,762
in the same young solar system,
at nearly the same time.

166
00:09:31,302 --> 00:09:36,342
From a distance, it is the closest
thing to a second Earth that we know.

167
00:09:37,102 --> 00:09:39,682
It is also closer to the Sun.

168
00:09:40,222 --> 00:09:44,182
Venus receives almost twice
the sunlight that reaches us.

169
00:09:44,762 --> 00:09:49,482
So the easy answer is that it
is simply nearer the fire.

170
00:09:50,262 --> 00:09:53,482
But that answer breaks on one number.

171
00:09:54,082 --> 00:09:58,382
Venus is wrapped in bright
cloud that reflects about

172
00:09:58,382 --> 00:10:02,242
three-quarters of that sunlight
straight back to space.

173
00:10:02,742 --> 00:10:06,802
So Venus actually soaks up
less sunlight than Earth does.

174
00:10:07,242 --> 00:10:12,742
Less light in, and still a surface at
four hundred and sixty-five degrees.

175
00:10:13,402 --> 00:10:15,202
It sounds like a paradox.

176
00:10:15,602 --> 00:10:18,862
It stops being one when
you look at the air.

177
00:10:19,902 --> 00:10:23,342
So the heat is not about
how much light arrives.

178
00:10:23,422 --> 00:10:26,702
It is about what happens to
the heat once it is there.

179
00:10:27,142 --> 00:10:29,282
It cannot get out.

180
00:10:30,202 --> 00:10:32,222
What would you see if you stood there?

181
00:10:32,802 --> 00:10:34,202
Not darkness.

182
00:10:34,462 --> 00:10:37,243
The clouds let through a dim, orange

183
00:10:37,243 --> 00:10:40,422
daylight, like a heavy
evening that never ends.

184
00:10:40,941 --> 00:10:43,262
You would never see the Sun itself.

185
00:10:43,822 --> 00:10:47,862
The wind at the ground moves at
little more than a slow walk,

186
00:10:47,942 --> 00:10:52,842
but in air that thick, even a
slow wind pushes like a river.

187
00:10:53,222 --> 00:10:57,122
The air of Venus is ninety-six
percent carbon dioxide,

188
00:10:57,202 --> 00:10:58,862
and there is a great deal of it.

189
00:10:59,262 --> 00:11:04,242
Carbon dioxide lets sunlight through
but blocks the heat trying to leave.

190
00:11:04,562 --> 00:11:10,582
On Earth it is a thin trace in the air,
and even that trace keeps the planet warm.

191
00:11:10,942 --> 00:11:16,842
On Venus the whole sky is made of
it, ninety times thicker than ours.

192
00:11:17,082 --> 00:11:18,602
The heat goes in.

193
00:11:18,622 --> 00:11:20,382
It does not come out.

194
00:11:21,322 --> 00:11:24,162
Day and night, pole and equator, the

195
00:11:24,162 --> 00:11:27,002
ground sits at nearly
the same temperature.

196
00:11:27,802 --> 00:11:29,202
Here is the turn.

197
00:11:29,642 --> 00:11:32,942
Earth has roughly as much
carbon dioxide as Venus.

198
00:11:32,962 --> 00:11:34,842
It is just not in the air.

199
00:11:34,882 --> 00:11:36,282
It is in rock.

200
00:11:36,402 --> 00:11:41,282
Limestone, chalk and seabed,
built over billions of years.

201
00:11:41,722 --> 00:11:44,362
Rain and rivers carry it to the sea,

202
00:11:44,422 --> 00:11:47,742
and sea life and chemistry
lock it into the floor.

203
00:11:48,402 --> 00:11:52,242
The thing that pulled Earth's
carbon out of the sky was water.

204
00:11:52,582 --> 00:11:54,402
So the question changes.

205
00:11:54,482 --> 00:11:58,042
It is not why Venus has
so much carbon dioxide.

206
00:11:58,222 --> 00:12:02,262
It is why Venus has no
water to put it away.

207
00:12:02,502 --> 00:12:04,162
The water is the mystery.

208
00:12:04,222 --> 00:12:07,882
And Venus left a clue in
the air about where it went.

209
00:12:08,342 --> 00:12:11,882
In 1978 a NASA probe
fell through the clouds

210
00:12:11,882 --> 00:12:15,322
of Venus and tasted the
air on the way down.

211
00:12:15,702 --> 00:12:19,822
It found a trace of water,
and something odd inside it.

212
00:12:20,402 --> 00:12:22,882
Some hydrogen is heavier than the rest.

213
00:12:22,902 --> 00:12:24,802
It is called deuterium.

214
00:12:25,422 --> 00:12:28,162
On Venus, the share of heavy hydrogen is

215
00:12:28,162 --> 00:12:30,882
around a hundred times
higher than on Earth.

216
00:12:31,222 --> 00:12:33,242
That number is a fingerprint.

217
00:12:33,782 --> 00:12:37,282
High in the air, sunlight
breaks water apart.

218
00:12:37,422 --> 00:12:41,802
The light hydrogen escapes to space
more easily than the heavy kind.

219
00:12:42,321 --> 00:12:46,101
Lose enough water that way,
over a long enough time,

220
00:12:46,102 --> 00:12:48,842
and the heavy hydrogen is what remains.

221
00:12:49,462 --> 00:12:52,182
Venus has lost a great deal of water.

222
00:12:52,562 --> 00:12:56,142
How much, and in what
form, is still unknown.

223
00:12:56,562 --> 00:12:58,262
Was it ever an ocean?

224
00:12:59,442 --> 00:13:00,882
Spin matters here.

225
00:13:01,242 --> 00:13:05,522
Venus turns once every two hundred
and forty-three Earth days,

226
00:13:05,602 --> 00:13:07,422
and it turns backwards.

227
00:13:08,022 --> 00:13:11,202
A single day there is
longer than its whole year.

228
00:13:11,722 --> 00:13:14,922
That slow turn decides
where the clouds gather.

229
00:13:15,522 --> 00:13:17,722
One model says it may have been.

230
00:13:18,142 --> 00:13:24,002
In 2016 a team at NASA's Goddard
Institute ran early Venus with that

231
00:13:24,002 --> 00:13:29,062
slow spin and found that thick
daytime clouds could shade the ground.

232
00:13:29,502 --> 00:13:32,182
In their model, Venus may have had shallow

233
00:13:32,182 --> 00:13:36,042
oceans and mild weather
for billions of years.

234
00:13:36,702 --> 00:13:39,802
Another model says the
water never came down.

235
00:13:40,062 --> 00:13:45,162
In 2021 a team led by Martin
Turbet found that clouds

236
00:13:45,162 --> 00:13:49,502
gathered on the night side
instead, trapping heat like a lid.

237
00:13:49,982 --> 00:13:54,622
The steam stayed in the sky and
the oceans may never have formed.

238
00:13:55,082 --> 00:13:58,282
Both are careful models,
and they disagree.

239
00:13:58,802 --> 00:14:02,082
Nobody has yet read the
rocks that could settle it.

240
00:14:02,722 --> 00:14:06,182
Either way, Venus ended in the same place.

241
00:14:06,442 --> 00:14:07,842
Water gone.

242
00:14:07,842 --> 00:14:09,422
Carbon in the sky.

243
00:14:09,622 --> 00:14:11,642
Heat with nowhere to go.

244
00:14:12,062 --> 00:14:14,262
So why Venus and not Earth?

245
00:14:14,662 --> 00:14:16,322
Distance is part of it.

246
00:14:16,542 --> 00:14:19,502
Nearer the Sun, there is more
light to start the climb.

247
00:14:19,682 --> 00:14:25,522
Warmer air holds more water vapour, and
water vapour is itself a greenhouse gas.

248
00:14:25,822 --> 00:14:30,582
Past a certain line, the steam reaches
high enough for sunlight to split it,

249
00:14:30,602 --> 00:14:33,602
and the water starts
leaking away to space.

250
00:14:34,222 --> 00:14:37,042
Venus sits on the wrong side of that line.

251
00:14:37,322 --> 00:14:40,482
Earth sits on the safe side, for now.

252
00:14:41,442 --> 00:14:44,002
Last week we saw the Sun getting brighter,

253
00:14:44,002 --> 00:14:47,562
about one percent every
hundred and ten million years.

254
00:14:47,802 --> 00:14:50,262
That climb moves the line outward.

255
00:14:50,482 --> 00:14:53,262
In about a billion
years, it reaches Earth.

256
00:14:53,622 --> 00:14:56,082
This is not a warning about next century.

257
00:14:56,142 --> 00:15:00,622
The warming measured on Earth today is
real, and it is a different ledger.

258
00:15:01,162 --> 00:15:04,162
Studies of Earth's climate
find that burning our fossil

259
00:15:04,162 --> 00:15:08,402
fuels is very unlikely to
start a full runaway like this.

260
00:15:09,022 --> 00:15:13,002
Venus is a story told in
hundreds of millions of years.

261
00:15:13,342 --> 00:15:16,442
What it shows is how
thin the difference is.

262
00:15:16,602 --> 00:15:21,002
Same rock, same start, and
one turn with the water.

263
00:15:21,402 --> 00:15:23,822
The answer is waiting on the ground.

264
00:15:24,622 --> 00:15:29,282
Venus has repainted most of its surface
with lava in the last billion years or so.

265
00:15:29,462 --> 00:15:33,422
But some high, crumpled regions
called tesserae look older.

266
00:15:34,022 --> 00:15:38,522
If water ever shaped Venus, rocks
like those may still remember it.

267
00:15:41,222 --> 00:15:45,202
In 1982 a Soviet lander
photographed the ground

268
00:15:45,202 --> 00:15:48,102
and lasted about two
hours before the heat won.

269
00:15:48,682 --> 00:15:53,542
In 2021 a probe on its way to the
Sun looked at the night side of

270
00:15:53,542 --> 00:15:57,842
Venus and saw the faint glow of
the hot surface through the clouds.

271
00:15:58,562 --> 00:16:01,242
The next step is to read
the rocks properly.

272
00:16:01,962 --> 00:16:05,722
NASA chose two missions
to go back: DAVINCI,

273
00:16:05,722 --> 00:16:08,032
designed to drop through the air and

274
00:16:08,032 --> 00:16:10,342
photograph the old
highlands of Alpha Regio,

275
00:16:10,462 --> 00:16:15,402
and VERITAS, designed to map the
surface and its rock types from orbit.

276
00:16:15,982 --> 00:16:18,722
Europe has its own orbiter, EnVision.

277
00:16:19,462 --> 00:16:22,062
The planet next door
is the clearest record

278
00:16:22,062 --> 00:16:25,322
we have of how a world like ours can turn.

279
00:16:25,742 --> 00:16:27,402
Not by falling apart.

280
00:16:27,622 --> 00:16:30,942
By keeping its heat, and losing its water.

281
00:16:32,142 --> 00:16:34,502
What if Venus was never the monster,

282
00:16:34,561 --> 00:16:38,102
but a twin that simply
ran the experiment first?

283
00:16:38,862 --> 00:16:41,602
The bigger question hangs in your own sky,

284
00:16:41,722 --> 00:16:44,922
the brightest thing at dawn
or dusk after the Moon.

285
00:16:45,522 --> 00:16:48,502
How close did Earth come to the same turn?

286
00:16:48,609 --> 00:16:51,589
On Venus, 92 bars of crushing heat

287
00:16:51,589 --> 00:16:55,329
silenced our toughest lander
in just over two hours.

288
00:16:56,249 --> 00:17:01,589
Head the other way from the Sun—
225 million kilometres out to Mars—

289
00:17:01,589 --> 00:17:06,209
and the problem flips:
an air so thin and cold

290
00:17:06,209 --> 00:17:09,169
that our robots can roam
for over a decade.

291
00:17:12,255 --> 00:17:17,775
A robot built to last ninety days on Mars
kept going for more than fourteen years.

292
00:17:18,215 --> 00:17:21,695
Why did it survive so long,
and what finally stopped it?

293
00:17:22,615 --> 00:17:24,835
Mars does not crush machines.

294
00:17:24,935 --> 00:17:26,335
It does not melt them.

295
00:17:26,535 --> 00:17:28,754
It wears them down, slowly,

296
00:17:28,775 --> 00:17:33,455
in ways you already know from home:
the cold that kills a phone battery,

297
00:17:33,535 --> 00:17:37,855
the dust on a windscreen,
a tyre that finally splits.

298
00:17:38,415 --> 00:17:40,595
So could a robot survive on Mars?

299
00:17:40,775 --> 00:17:41,895
Could Orbit?

300
00:17:41,895 --> 00:17:46,175
At first glance, Mars looks like
a desert you could drive across.

301
00:17:46,315 --> 00:17:49,595
Red rock, rolling dunes, a hazy sky.

302
00:17:49,835 --> 00:17:51,555
It is a trick of the eye.

303
00:17:51,715 --> 00:17:54,375
The air is so thin that
the pressure at the

304
00:17:54,375 --> 00:17:57,335
ground is less than
one percent of Earth's.

305
00:17:57,735 --> 00:18:01,475
The average temperature is
about minus sixty degrees.

306
00:18:01,975 --> 00:18:07,035
At the first Viking landing site,
nights fell to nearly minus ninety.

307
00:18:07,515 --> 00:18:12,135
And because the air is thin and
Mars has no global magnetic shield,

308
00:18:12,175 --> 00:18:14,375
radiation reaches the ground.

309
00:18:14,815 --> 00:18:16,535
Curiosity measured it.

310
00:18:16,735 --> 00:18:19,825
In one day on Mars, the dose is roughly

311
00:18:19,825 --> 00:18:22,915
what you would get in
three months on Earth.

312
00:18:23,435 --> 00:18:26,755
A person would need a pressure
suit just to stand there.

313
00:18:27,555 --> 00:18:31,615
But a robot does not breathe,
and it does not get sick.

314
00:18:32,115 --> 00:18:38,175
So the thin air and the radiation are not
what usually ends a robot's life on Mars.

315
00:18:38,695 --> 00:18:40,595
The killers are quieter.

316
00:18:41,095 --> 00:18:42,495
Cold.

317
00:18:42,575 --> 00:18:43,975
Dust.

318
00:18:44,175 --> 00:18:45,575
Wear.

319
00:18:45,815 --> 00:18:49,095
Of the robots that landed
safely and went to work,

320
00:18:49,175 --> 00:18:51,715
most were stopped by one of those three.

321
00:18:52,135 --> 00:18:55,495
And whatever goes wrong,
nobody can rush to help.

322
00:18:55,875 --> 00:18:59,515
A radio signal takes from
about three minutes to more

323
00:18:59,515 --> 00:19:03,695
than twenty to cross between
the planets, each way.

324
00:19:04,495 --> 00:19:08,175
Nobody can steer a rover
live, like a toy car.

325
00:19:08,975 --> 00:19:13,935
Every robot on Mars has to look after
itself between messages from home.

326
00:19:14,395 --> 00:19:17,715
Start with the cold, because
you have felt what it does.

327
00:19:17,935 --> 00:19:21,915
Take your phone out on a freezing
day and watch the battery drop.

328
00:19:22,095 --> 00:19:25,015
Electronics and batteries hate deep cold.

329
00:19:25,595 --> 00:19:30,515
On Mars, every night is colder than
any winter night in a British garden.

330
00:19:30,655 --> 00:19:35,115
So a rover spends part of its power
just keeping its own insides warm,

331
00:19:35,115 --> 00:19:37,875
like a person burning energy to shiver.

332
00:19:38,535 --> 00:19:41,355
That only works while
power keeps coming in.

333
00:19:42,035 --> 00:19:46,515
In 2009 the rover Spirit
drove into soft sand at

334
00:19:46,515 --> 00:19:50,195
a place the team called
Troy, and its wheels sank.

335
00:19:50,495 --> 00:19:52,075
It could not climb out.

336
00:19:52,195 --> 00:19:56,515
It could not tilt its solar panels
towards the low winter Sun.

337
00:19:57,115 --> 00:20:00,015
As the days shortened, its power fell.

338
00:20:01,015 --> 00:20:06,235
On the twenty-second of March
2010, Spirit sent its last message.

339
00:20:07,015 --> 00:20:09,035
Spirit did not break apart.

340
00:20:09,255 --> 00:20:12,635
It got stuck, and winter did the rest.

341
00:20:13,515 --> 00:20:16,235
Its mission had been
planned for ninety days.

342
00:20:16,255 --> 00:20:18,915
It worked for more than six years.

343
00:20:20,075 --> 00:20:22,495
The cold can be even more direct.

344
00:20:22,755 --> 00:20:25,673
In 2008 the Phoenix
lander touched down near

345
00:20:25,673 --> 00:20:28,955
the north pole of Mars to dig for ice.

346
00:20:29,415 --> 00:20:32,095
When winter came, it went silent.

347
00:20:32,775 --> 00:20:36,835
Two years later, a spacecraft
in orbit photographed it again.

348
00:20:37,035 --> 00:20:39,255
Its solar panels looked broken,

349
00:20:39,295 --> 00:20:44,675
most likely bent by the weight of frost
and ice that had built up over the winter.

350
00:20:45,315 --> 00:20:48,155
Spirit had a twin on the far side of Mars.

351
00:20:48,215 --> 00:20:52,215
It lasted much longer, and
the reason was the wind.

352
00:20:52,695 --> 00:20:54,715
Mars is a dusty world.

353
00:20:54,835 --> 00:20:58,895
Fine red dust settles on
everything, including solar panels.

354
00:20:59,055 --> 00:21:01,135
A panel under dust is like a windscreen

355
00:21:01,135 --> 00:21:04,515
nobody wipes: less and
less light gets through.

356
00:21:04,995 --> 00:21:08,175
A rover that runs on
sunlight slowly starves.

357
00:21:08,435 --> 00:21:10,934
Opportunity should have
starved within months.

358
00:21:10,974 --> 00:21:12,575
Instead, again and again,

359
00:21:12,575 --> 00:21:17,015
Martian wind and passing dust
devils swept its panels clean.

360
00:21:17,395 --> 00:21:19,655
The team called them cleaning events.

361
00:21:19,755 --> 00:21:23,375
Each one gave the rover back
its power, and it kept driving.

362
00:21:23,635 --> 00:21:25,735
Ninety days became years.

363
00:21:25,875 --> 00:21:28,115
Years became more than fourteen.

364
00:21:28,715 --> 00:21:33,055
Then, in 2018, a storm grew until
it wrapped the whole planet.

365
00:21:33,195 --> 00:21:38,895
The sky over Opportunity grew so dark that
almost no sunlight reached its panels.

366
00:21:39,455 --> 00:21:44,135
On the tenth of June 2018,
Opportunity sent its last signal.

367
00:21:44,595 --> 00:21:47,355
Engineers on Earth sent more
than a thousand commands

368
00:21:47,355 --> 00:21:50,475
over the next eight months,
asking it to wake up.

369
00:21:50,775 --> 00:21:52,175
It never answered.

370
00:21:52,955 --> 00:21:58,355
What finally stopped the most successful
rover of its time was not cold or rock.

371
00:21:58,535 --> 00:22:00,855
It was the sky turning off.

372
00:22:02,075 --> 00:22:04,155
Why does dust matter so much?

373
00:22:04,415 --> 00:22:08,035
Because on Mars there is no
rain to wash anything clean.

374
00:22:08,415 --> 00:22:11,735
The lander InSight spent four
years listening for marsquakes,

375
00:22:12,515 --> 00:22:14,715
and felt more than a thousand of them.

376
00:22:14,935 --> 00:22:17,395
But dust kept building on its panels.

377
00:22:17,755 --> 00:22:24,535
In December 2022, it went quiet the same
way: not broken, just out of light.

378
00:22:25,135 --> 00:22:28,075
Even a robot that never runs
out of power wears out.

379
00:22:28,475 --> 00:22:31,595
Curiosity runs on a nuclear power source,

380
00:22:31,715 --> 00:22:37,355
a small supply of plutonium that gives
off heat day and night, dust or no dust.

381
00:22:37,955 --> 00:22:41,455
When the 2018 storm
darkened Opportunity's sky,

382
00:22:41,615 --> 00:22:44,695
Curiosity kept working in
another part of the planet.

383
00:22:44,835 --> 00:22:46,875
But its wheels had a different problem.

384
00:22:47,075 --> 00:22:51,435
They are thin aluminium, made
light enough to fly to Mars.

385
00:22:51,615 --> 00:22:54,575
In 2013, the team noticed holes.

386
00:22:54,815 --> 00:22:58,955
Sharp rocks were punching through,
like a tyre on broken glass.

387
00:22:59,379 --> 00:23:03,619
Since then the drivers have planned
routes around the sharpest ground,

388
00:23:03,679 --> 00:23:06,819
and they photograph the wheels
to watch the damage grow.

389
00:23:07,419 --> 00:23:09,439
Why not just build the wheels tougher?

390
00:23:09,799 --> 00:23:14,299
Because every kilogram has to be
launched off Earth, carried for months,

391
00:23:14,319 --> 00:23:19,439
slowed from thousands of kilometres an
hour and set down gently on another world.

392
00:23:19,599 --> 00:23:24,259
On Mars, strength always has to be
weighed against getting there at all.

393
00:23:24,899 --> 00:23:27,099
And then there is the little helicopter.

394
00:23:27,559 --> 00:23:31,939
Ingenuity was built for up to five
test flights over thirty days,

395
00:23:31,939 --> 00:23:34,739
in air one percent as thick as ours.

396
00:23:35,079 --> 00:23:38,879
It flew seventy-two times
over almost three years.

397
00:23:39,259 --> 00:23:45,999
In January 2024, after its last landing,
images showed damage to its rotor blades.

398
00:23:46,139 --> 00:23:47,799
It could not fly again.

399
00:23:48,259 --> 00:23:50,279
It is still standing on Mars.

400
00:23:50,759 --> 00:23:53,639
So, could a robot survive on Mars?

401
00:23:53,899 --> 00:23:54,899
Yes.

402
00:23:54,899 --> 00:23:56,939
Not forever, and not alone.

403
00:23:57,259 --> 00:24:01,219
The robots that last are the ones
with a steady supply of power,

404
00:24:01,279 --> 00:24:03,059
heaters to fight the night,

405
00:24:03,059 --> 00:24:07,339
and a team on Earth who drive
carefully around every sharp rock.

406
00:24:07,779 --> 00:24:10,198
Sometimes they also
need luck, in the shape

407
00:24:10,198 --> 00:24:12,919
of a gust of wind at the right moment.

408
00:24:13,199 --> 00:24:15,639
That is what Mars teaches.

409
00:24:15,759 --> 00:24:18,899
Survival there is not one heroic moment.

410
00:24:18,959 --> 00:24:24,959
It is a thousand small decisions, made
from another planet, every single day.

411
00:24:25,519 --> 00:24:26,979
What would Orbit need?

412
00:24:27,179 --> 00:24:30,139
Power that does not depend on a clear sky.

413
00:24:30,259 --> 00:24:32,799
A way to stay warm through the night.

414
00:24:32,919 --> 00:24:35,759
A shell tough enough for sharp ground.

415
00:24:35,879 --> 00:24:38,299
And someone at home, watching.

416
00:24:38,659 --> 00:24:43,499
What if the real story of every
Mars robot is not how it died,

417
00:24:43,539 --> 00:24:46,099
but how long people kept it going?

418
00:24:46,299 --> 00:24:49,379
The bigger question sits
in the tracks they leave.

419
00:24:49,599 --> 00:24:54,339
Every robot that stops on Mars
stays exactly where it stopped,

420
00:24:54,339 --> 00:24:56,795
and the dust slowly covers it.

421
00:24:56,935 --> 00:25:00,515
Beyond the rusty plains of Mars
and the giant planets,

422
00:25:01,055 --> 00:25:05,695
the Sun's gravity still holds
a vast shell of frozen comets

423
00:25:05,695 --> 00:25:10,315
10,000 to 100,000 times
further out than Earth.

424
00:25:11,455 --> 00:25:17,315
And out there in the dark, another star
is already heading our way.

425
00:25:20,065 --> 00:25:22,305
There is a star coming towards us.

426
00:25:22,945 --> 00:25:25,545
So how close will it get, and should you
worry?

427
00:25:26,585 --> 00:25:28,785
This is not a guess and not a rumour.

428
00:25:29,305 --> 00:25:33,125
The star has been measured, and it is
heading almost straight at the sun.

429
00:25:34,085 --> 00:25:37,145
Its name is Gliese 710.

430
00:25:37,965 --> 00:25:43,245
In about one point three million years, it
will pass closer to us than any star we

431
00:25:43,285 --> 00:25:44,085
know of.

432
00:25:44,605 --> 00:25:49,565
It will not hit anything, but it will sail
right through the outer edge of our solar

433
00:25:49,665 --> 00:25:50,465
system.

434
00:25:50,825 --> 00:25:55,545
Right now, Gliese 710 is about 62
light-years away in the

435
00:25:55,585 --> 00:25:58,145
constellation of Serpens, the serpent.

436
00:25:59,085 --> 00:26:04,565
It is an orange dwarf, a little smaller
and cooler than the sun, with about 60%

437
00:26:04,605 --> 00:26:05,585
of the sun's mass.

438
00:26:06,685 --> 00:26:07,565
You cannot see it.

439
00:26:08,145 --> 00:26:11,625
It is far too faint for your eyes on even
the darkest night.

440
00:26:12,705 --> 00:26:14,565
You would need binoculars just to find it.

441
00:26:16,065 --> 00:26:17,045
So why does it matter?

442
00:26:18,085 --> 00:26:19,685
Because of the way it moves.

443
00:26:20,685 --> 00:26:25,765
Most stars drift slowly sideways across
our sky year after year.

444
00:26:27,065 --> 00:26:29,965
Gliese 710 hardly drifts at all.

445
00:26:31,045 --> 00:26:36,285
It is coming towards us at about 14
kilometres every second, more than

446
00:26:36,505 --> 00:26:41,685
50,000 kilometres an hour, and almost none
of that motion is sideways.

447
00:26:43,125 --> 00:26:47,825
When something barely moves across your
view but keeps getting closer, it is

448
00:26:47,865 --> 00:26:48,665
heading for you,

449
00:26:49,445 --> 00:26:52,625
like a car on a long, straight road at
night.

450
00:26:53,325 --> 00:26:55,645
Its headlights do not slide left or right.

451
00:26:56,105 --> 00:26:57,245
They just grow.

452
00:26:58,365 --> 00:27:03,825
If you saw our film on the nearest star,
you met Proxima Centauri about four

453
00:27:03,885 --> 00:27:04,765
light-years away.

454
00:27:05,805 --> 00:27:08,245
Proxima is our nearest neighbour today,

455
00:27:09,325 --> 00:27:14,085
but stars move, and the title of nearest
does not last forever.

456
00:27:14,945 --> 00:27:17,145
How do we know where it will be in a
million years?

457
00:27:17,684 --> 00:27:19,065
For a long time, we did not.

458
00:27:19,745 --> 00:27:21,885
We were not even sure it would come close
at all.

459
00:27:22,625 --> 00:27:27,825
In the late 1990s, a satellite called
Hipparcos measured the star's position and

460
00:27:27,905 --> 00:27:28,705
motion.

461
00:27:29,105 --> 00:27:33,345
The best guess then was that it would pass
about a light-year from the sun, and the

462
00:27:33,405 --> 00:27:35,025
uncertainty was huge.

463
00:27:35,805 --> 00:27:40,405
Then came Gaia, the European spacecraft
that has measured more than a billion

464
00:27:40,565 --> 00:27:41,365
stars.

465
00:27:41,845 --> 00:27:47,145
With every new release of its data, the
path came into sharper focus, and the

466
00:27:47,225 --> 00:27:49,085
star kept coming closer.

467
00:27:50,105 --> 00:27:53,765
The latest Gaia measurements put the
closest approach at about a fifth of a

468
00:27:53,825 --> 00:27:54,625
light-year.

469
00:27:55,025 --> 00:27:59,965
That is about 10,000 to 13,000 times
further than Earth is from the sun,

470
00:28:00,605 --> 00:28:02,845
five times closer than the old estimate.

471
00:28:03,545 --> 00:28:05,985
That still sounds far, so let's shrink it.

472
00:28:06,405 --> 00:28:09,485
Make the gap between the sun and Earth one
centimetre.

473
00:28:10,225 --> 00:28:15,625
Neptune, our outermost planet, is then 30
centimetres away, about the length of a

474
00:28:15,725 --> 00:28:16,525
ruler.

475
00:28:16,745 --> 00:28:22,005
And Gliese 710 passes more than 100 metres
away, the length of a

476
00:28:22,045 --> 00:28:22,865
football pitch.

477
00:28:23,725 --> 00:28:26,565
So the star will not come anywhere near
the planets.

478
00:28:27,005 --> 00:28:29,525
But what is out there 100 metres from the
sun?

479
00:28:30,145 --> 00:28:31,805
Something we have never seen.

480
00:28:32,285 --> 00:28:36,345
Far beyond the planets, the sun is wrapped
in a vast cloud of ice.

481
00:28:37,185 --> 00:28:42,585
Trillions of frozen bodies, the leftovers
from when the planets formed, drifting so

482
00:28:42,625 --> 00:28:45,625
far out that the sun's pull on them is
barely a whisper.

483
00:28:46,645 --> 00:28:51,125
It is called the Oort cloud, and it is
still one of the great unknowns of our

484
00:28:51,185 --> 00:28:52,005
solar system.

485
00:28:52,785 --> 00:28:54,785
Nobody has ever seen it directly.

486
00:28:56,105 --> 00:29:00,545
We know it is there because of the comets
that fall out of it on journeys that take

487
00:29:00,585 --> 00:29:02,985
them hundreds of thousands of years or
more.

488
00:29:03,825 --> 00:29:08,225
Its inner edge is thought to start a few
thousand times further out than Earth.

489
00:29:08,825 --> 00:29:11,785
Its outer edge may reach 100,000.

490
00:29:13,125 --> 00:29:17,545
Sunlight, which gets to us in eight
minutes, would take months to reach the

491
00:29:17,585 --> 00:29:22,685
place where Gliese 710 will pass, and that
is exactly where the star is

492
00:29:22,745 --> 00:29:23,545
going,

493
00:29:23,985 --> 00:29:25,945
straight through the Oort cloud.

494
00:29:26,805 --> 00:29:31,705
A star passing through the cloud is like a
hand brushing through a bowl of floating

495
00:29:31,805 --> 00:29:32,605
marbles.

496
00:29:33,225 --> 00:29:36,005
Its gravity nudges the comets nearest its
path.

497
00:29:36,385 --> 00:29:38,485
Some get flung out into the galaxy.

498
00:29:39,045 --> 00:29:42,525
Others are knocked onto new orbits that
fall in towards the sun.

499
00:29:43,685 --> 00:29:49,005
The calculation suggests that Gliese 710
will send about 10 extra comets a year,

500
00:29:49,445 --> 00:29:54,265
falling in close enough for us to see, and
it will keep happening for three to four

501
00:29:54,445 --> 00:29:55,465
million years.

502
00:29:56,425 --> 00:29:58,405
But here is the strange part.

503
00:29:59,045 --> 00:30:01,985
The comets do not arrive while the star is
passing.

504
00:30:02,805 --> 00:30:03,605
Why?

505
00:30:04,105 --> 00:30:09,025
Because they are so far out that falling
in takes hundreds of thousands of years.

506
00:30:09,745 --> 00:30:14,625
By the time the shower is at its height,
Gliese 710 will already be heading away

507
00:30:14,705 --> 00:30:17,405
again, shrinking back into the dark.

508
00:30:18,565 --> 00:30:19,565
Should anyone worry?

509
00:30:20,585 --> 00:30:21,385
Not much.

510
00:30:21,845 --> 00:30:24,585
Most comets that fall in never come close
to Earth.

511
00:30:25,105 --> 00:30:29,185
Jupiter and Saturn throw many of them back
out or steer them away.

512
00:30:30,165 --> 00:30:35,585
What the people of that far future get is
a sky with more comets in it for a

513
00:30:35,645 --> 00:30:36,945
very long time.

514
00:30:37,785 --> 00:30:38,585
So what would you see?

515
00:30:39,305 --> 00:30:42,305
Imagine standing on Earth on the night of
the closest pass.

516
00:30:43,025 --> 00:30:46,045
A new star has been brightening for
thousands of years.

517
00:30:46,845 --> 00:30:51,485
Now it is the brightest star in the whole
night sky, about three times brighter than

518
00:30:51,525 --> 00:30:56,345
Sirius and as bright as Jupiter at its
best, and it glows orange.

519
00:30:57,365 --> 00:31:00,845
It will not light up the night like the
moon, and it will not warm anyone.

520
00:31:01,345 --> 00:31:05,465
It is still billions of times fainter than
our sun, but it will move.

521
00:31:06,445 --> 00:31:08,665
Most stars look fixed for a lifetime.

522
00:31:09,365 --> 00:31:14,365
This one will creep across the sky by
about the width of the full moon every 35

523
00:31:14,445 --> 00:31:15,245
years.

524
00:31:15,545 --> 00:31:19,245
Then, slowly, it will fade and drift away
for good.

525
00:31:20,245 --> 00:31:22,905
Is this the first time a star has come
this close?

526
00:31:23,745 --> 00:31:24,545
Not at all.

527
00:31:25,185 --> 00:31:28,725
Gliese 710 will not be the first star to
pass this way.

528
00:31:29,665 --> 00:31:35,065
About 70,000 years ago, a dim red pair of
stars, now called Scholz's

529
00:31:35,145 --> 00:31:40,305
Star, skimmed through the outer Oort cloud
less than a light-year from the sun.

530
00:31:41,305 --> 00:31:44,465
It was too small and too faint to stir up
much.

531
00:31:45,245 --> 00:31:50,725
And about 2.8 million years ago, a star
much like our sun, called

532
00:31:50,985 --> 00:31:56,345
HD 7977, may have passed as close as
Gliese 710

533
00:31:56,405 --> 00:31:57,205
will.

534
00:31:57,965 --> 00:32:03,245
Gaia's measurements cannot yet pin down
exactly how close, so this is not a rare

535
00:32:03,285 --> 00:32:04,925
disaster waiting to happen.

536
00:32:05,705 --> 00:32:07,785
It is how life works in a galaxy.

537
00:32:08,685 --> 00:32:13,945
The sun is one star among billions, all of
them moving, and every few

538
00:32:13,985 --> 00:32:16,725
million years, one of them brushes past.

539
00:32:18,805 --> 00:32:24,025
What if the night sky you have always
known is not fixed at all, but a slow

540
00:32:24,185 --> 00:32:26,665
traffic of stars still on the move?

541
00:32:27,845 --> 00:32:30,105
The bigger question is the cloud itself.

542
00:32:31,125 --> 00:32:36,265
Some astronomers think the sun was born in
a crowd of sister stars, and that many of

543
00:32:36,305 --> 00:32:38,925
the comets in our Oort cloud were once
theirs.

544
00:32:39,905 --> 00:32:45,025
If they are right, every star that passes
is part of a very old exchange.

545
00:32:46,105 --> 00:32:51,285
Some of the ice falling in from the dark
may have started out around another sun.

546
00:32:51,828 --> 00:32:57,248
Gliese 710 will not reach our comet cloud
for another 1.3 million years.

547
00:32:57,248 --> 00:33:01,468
But right now, just beyond
that outer shell of ice,

548
00:33:01,468 --> 00:33:04,388
sits our true nearest neighbour
in the galaxy—

549
00:33:05,128 --> 00:33:09,648
a dim red star 40 trillion kilometres
across the dark.

550
00:33:12,428 --> 00:33:15,568
Look up on a clear night
and the stars seem close,

551
00:33:15,588 --> 00:33:17,688
scattered just above the rooftops.

552
00:33:17,968 --> 00:33:20,188
So how far away is the nearest one?

553
00:33:20,888 --> 00:33:25,928
Light, the fastest thing there is, takes
more than four years to cross the gap.

554
00:33:26,248 --> 00:33:28,008
And here is the strange part.

555
00:33:28,228 --> 00:33:30,248
You cannot see that star at all.

556
00:33:30,508 --> 00:33:32,088
Not without a telescope.

557
00:33:33,088 --> 00:33:37,348
The nearest star to the Sun
is called Proxima Centauri.

558
00:33:37,708 --> 00:33:43,828
It is a red dwarf, a small, cool star,
far smaller and dimmer than ours.

559
00:33:44,128 --> 00:33:49,188
That is why you will never spot it
by eye, even on the darkest night.

560
00:33:49,688 --> 00:33:53,028
It gives out less than one
percent of the Sun's light.

561
00:33:53,328 --> 00:33:56,228
But small stars burn their fuel slowly.

562
00:33:56,528 --> 00:34:00,308
Proxima will still be glowing
for trillions of years,

563
00:34:00,448 --> 00:34:03,148
long after our own Sun has gone.

564
00:34:03,848 --> 00:34:05,628
But it is not alone.

565
00:34:06,128 --> 00:34:13,008
Close beside it in the sky are two much
brighter stars, Alpha Centauri A and B,

566
00:34:13,228 --> 00:34:18,568
a pair a little further off at about
four point three seven light-years.

567
00:34:19,228 --> 00:34:21,788
From the southern hemisphere
they shine as one

568
00:34:21,788 --> 00:34:24,888
of the brightest points
in the whole night sky.

569
00:34:25,108 --> 00:34:27,748
From Britain, they never rise.

570
00:34:28,428 --> 00:34:30,488
The three are one family.

571
00:34:30,688 --> 00:34:34,788
Proxima loops around the bright
pair at a vast distance,

572
00:34:34,848 --> 00:34:38,668
and one orbit takes it
about half a million years.

573
00:34:39,328 --> 00:34:41,848
When anyone says "the nearest star",

574
00:34:41,948 --> 00:34:47,688
they mean the faint one: Proxima,
four point two four light-years away.

575
00:34:48,448 --> 00:34:51,828
A light-year is a distance, not a time.

576
00:34:52,048 --> 00:34:55,028
It is how far light travels in a year.

577
00:34:55,428 --> 00:34:57,488
So when you look towards Proxima,

578
00:34:57,548 --> 00:35:01,268
you are seeing light that left
it more than four years ago.

579
00:35:01,688 --> 00:35:05,248
How do you measure the distance
to something you can never visit?

580
00:35:06,128 --> 00:35:07,528
Try this.

581
00:35:07,648 --> 00:35:10,288
Hold up your thumb at arm's length.

582
00:35:10,808 --> 00:35:13,348
Close one eye, then the other.

583
00:35:14,068 --> 00:35:16,708
Your thumb jumps against the background.

584
00:35:17,028 --> 00:35:20,148
The closer your thumb,
the bigger the jump.

585
00:35:20,948 --> 00:35:23,308
Your two eyes are a ruler.

586
00:35:23,908 --> 00:35:28,168
Astronomers use the same trick
with the whole of Earth's orbit.

587
00:35:28,648 --> 00:35:34,308
Photograph a star in January, and again
in July, from the other side of the Sun.

588
00:35:35,048 --> 00:35:41,628
A nearby star shifts a tiny amount against
the far more distant stars behind it.

589
00:35:42,248 --> 00:35:46,068
Measure the shift, and geometry
gives you the distance.

590
00:35:46,728 --> 00:35:49,528
The shift is unbelievably small.

591
00:35:49,848 --> 00:35:52,928
For centuries nobody could see it at all,

592
00:35:53,068 --> 00:35:57,608
and some astronomers took that as
proof that Earth must stand still.

593
00:35:58,428 --> 00:36:01,028
The real reason was the distance.

594
00:36:01,868 --> 00:36:09,668
In 1838 the astronomer Friedrich Bessel
measured it for a star called 61 Cygni.

595
00:36:10,228 --> 00:36:12,648
The angle he found was like the width of a

596
00:36:12,648 --> 00:36:16,288
coin seen from about
thirteen kilometres away.

597
00:36:16,988 --> 00:36:20,968
It was the first time anyone had
measured the distance to a star,

598
00:36:21,208 --> 00:36:24,408
and it showed how deep the sky really is.

599
00:36:25,328 --> 00:36:29,488
Today a European
spacecraft called Gaia has

600
00:36:29,488 --> 00:36:32,968
measured these shifts for
more than a billion stars.

601
00:36:33,648 --> 00:36:38,028
Proxima's is the largest of
all, because it is the nearest.

602
00:36:38,648 --> 00:36:43,788
Even so, it is like seeing a coin
from about five kilometres away.

603
00:36:44,408 --> 00:36:48,468
Numbers like four
light-years are hard to feel.

604
00:36:48,848 --> 00:36:50,908
So let's shrink everything down.

605
00:36:51,348 --> 00:36:54,928
Make the Sun the size of a
grapefruit, and put it on a table.

606
00:36:55,408 --> 00:37:01,408
At that scale, Earth is a pinhead about
fifteen metres away, across a big room.

607
00:37:01,928 --> 00:37:07,828
Everything you have ever known, every
person, every city, sits on that pin.

608
00:37:08,287 --> 00:37:10,928
The farthest any human has ever travelled,

609
00:37:10,928 --> 00:37:14,628
to the Moon, is only about
four centimetres from it.

610
00:37:15,308 --> 00:37:17,328
Now where is the nearest star?

611
00:37:17,828 --> 00:37:19,848
Proxima becomes a cherry.

612
00:37:20,008 --> 00:37:23,428
And that cherry is about four
thousand kilometres away.

613
00:37:24,008 --> 00:37:28,768
Put the grapefruit in London, and the
cherry is further away than Cairo.

614
00:37:29,248 --> 00:37:31,748
A little beyond it sit the bright pair,

615
00:37:31,788 --> 00:37:37,028
Alpha Centauri A and B: one about
the size of a big grapefruit,

616
00:37:37,028 --> 00:37:39,028
the other more like an orange.

617
00:37:39,328 --> 00:37:41,388
Between them, almost nothing.

618
00:37:41,648 --> 00:37:47,248
A few specks of dust, and an emptiness
so complete that it is hard to picture.

619
00:37:47,848 --> 00:37:49,908
And our farthest spacecraft?

620
00:37:50,348 --> 00:37:53,548
Voyager 1 launched in 1977.

621
00:37:53,808 --> 00:37:56,188
After nearly fifty years of flying,

622
00:37:56,188 --> 00:38:01,048
at this scale it has travelled about two
and a half kilometres from the grapefruit.

623
00:38:01,248 --> 00:38:03,208
It has barely left the town.

624
00:38:03,728 --> 00:38:06,248
So how long would the journey take?

625
00:38:06,708 --> 00:38:10,588
Walk at a steady pace, never
stopping, never sleeping,

626
00:38:10,708 --> 00:38:15,108
and you would reach Proxima in
about nine hundred million years.

627
00:38:15,468 --> 00:38:20,708
Take a car at motorway speed, and it
is about forty-six million years,

628
00:38:20,828 --> 00:38:23,468
far longer than humans have existed.

629
00:38:24,048 --> 00:38:28,008
A jet airliner cuts it to
about five million years.

630
00:38:28,428 --> 00:38:33,588
Voyager 1 is the fastest thing we have
ever sent out of the solar system,

631
00:38:33,728 --> 00:38:37,468
moving at about seventeen
kilometres every second.

632
00:38:37,608 --> 00:38:39,908
It is not heading towards Proxima.

633
00:38:39,928 --> 00:38:42,848
But at its speed, crossing
the same distance

634
00:38:42,848 --> 00:38:46,108
would take about
seventy-five thousand years.

635
00:38:46,668 --> 00:38:50,448
Even light, at three hundred
thousand kilometres a second,

636
00:38:50,468 --> 00:38:53,288
needs four years and three months.

637
00:38:53,608 --> 00:38:55,968
Put that beside the light you know.

638
00:38:56,372 --> 00:38:59,432
Moonlight reaches you
in just over a second.

639
00:38:59,632 --> 00:39:02,252
Sunlight takes about eight minutes.

640
00:39:02,492 --> 00:39:06,352
Light from the nearest star
takes more than four years,

641
00:39:06,352 --> 00:39:09,952
and that is the shortest
trip of its kind there is.

642
00:39:10,432 --> 00:39:13,052
So what is waiting at
the end of the journey?

643
00:39:13,352 --> 00:39:17,352
In 2016 astronomers found
a planet around Proxima.

644
00:39:17,832 --> 00:39:21,132
Proxima b is at least
about as massive as Earth,

645
00:39:21,272 --> 00:39:24,232
and it circles its star every eleven days,

646
00:39:24,292 --> 00:39:27,812
close enough to sit in the zone
where water could be liquid.

647
00:39:28,291 --> 00:39:30,692
But do not picture a second Earth yet.

648
00:39:30,852 --> 00:39:35,032
Nobody knows what its surface is
like, or whether it has air at all.

649
00:39:35,432 --> 00:39:37,492
Its star is a violent one.

650
00:39:37,532 --> 00:39:41,712
Proxima throws out flares,
sudden blasts of energy that,

651
00:39:41,712 --> 00:39:45,832
for a star its size, are far
more violent than our Sun's.

652
00:39:46,132 --> 00:39:48,912
They may have stripped
any atmosphere away.

653
00:39:49,572 --> 00:39:53,052
Whether Proxima b could ever
be friendly is still open.

654
00:39:53,352 --> 00:39:54,952
Could we ever go and look?

655
00:39:55,492 --> 00:39:58,432
One plan, called Breakthrough Starshot,

656
00:39:58,512 --> 00:40:01,911
imagines probes weighing
about as much as a paperclip,

657
00:40:01,932 --> 00:40:05,692
pushed by giant lasers to a
fifth of the speed of light,

658
00:40:05,752 --> 00:40:08,052
arriving in about twenty years.

659
00:40:08,352 --> 00:40:10,472
Nothing like it has been built yet.

660
00:40:10,572 --> 00:40:13,412
It is a plan, and a very hard one.

661
00:40:14,152 --> 00:40:16,612
Even a simple hello is slow.

662
00:40:16,952 --> 00:40:22,252
A radio message sent from Earth
tonight would arrive in early 2031.

663
00:40:22,732 --> 00:40:28,792
If someone there answered straight away,
the earliest reply would reach us in 2035.

664
00:40:29,312 --> 00:40:31,892
So this is the nearest star.

665
00:40:32,272 --> 00:40:34,872
A faint red sun you cannot see,

666
00:40:34,932 --> 00:40:39,832
more than four light-years away,
with a world we cannot yet know.

667
00:40:40,772 --> 00:40:42,792
What if the most important thing about the

668
00:40:42,792 --> 00:40:47,532
nearest star is not how
close it is, but how far?

669
00:40:48,192 --> 00:40:51,672
The bigger question sits
above you every clear night.

670
00:40:52,032 --> 00:40:55,912
Each of those points of light
is at least that far away,

671
00:40:56,112 --> 00:40:58,852
most of them far, far further.

672
00:40:59,252 --> 00:41:02,412
And we have only just
begun to measure the gap.

673
00:41:02,815 --> 00:41:06,035
At the speed of our fastest space probes,

674
00:41:06,035 --> 00:41:11,795
crossing even that first four-light-year
gap takes 75,000 years.

675
00:41:12,695 --> 00:41:16,075
To reach the stars inside
a single human lifetime,

676
00:41:16,775 --> 00:41:19,715
a ship would have to push toward
the speed of light itself—

677
00:41:20,015 --> 00:41:23,235
where time stops behaving
the way it does at home.

678
00:41:26,001 --> 00:41:29,401
You leave Earth at ninety-nine
percent of the speed of light.

679
00:41:29,661 --> 00:41:32,201
Why does home come back older than you?

680
00:41:33,001 --> 00:41:35,721
You are gone for one
year by your own watch.

681
00:41:35,781 --> 00:41:39,041
You land, and seven years
have passed at home.

682
00:41:39,221 --> 00:41:40,301
Nobody cheated.

683
00:41:40,301 --> 00:41:41,701
Your watch is fine.

684
00:41:41,881 --> 00:41:44,821
Time itself ran at a
different pace for you.

685
00:41:45,181 --> 00:41:47,281
The danger was never the speed.

686
00:41:47,421 --> 00:41:49,081
It was your own clock.

687
00:41:49,581 --> 00:41:51,481
That is not science fiction.

688
00:41:51,521 --> 00:41:54,121
Your phone already
depends on it every day.

689
00:41:54,121 --> 00:41:56,441
Start with the satellites over your head.

690
00:41:56,681 --> 00:42:02,041
Every GPS satellite carries an atomic
clock, and every one of them drifts.

691
00:42:02,041 --> 00:42:03,461
Because they move fast,

692
00:42:03,461 --> 00:42:08,341
their clocks run slow by about seven
millionths of a second each day.

693
00:42:08,441 --> 00:42:14,401
Because they sit higher up, in weaker
gravity, they run fast by about forty-six.

694
00:42:14,461 --> 00:42:19,301
The total is about thirty-eight
millionths of a second a day, gained.

695
00:42:19,481 --> 00:42:20,881
That sounds like nothing.

696
00:42:20,901 --> 00:42:23,961
But your position comes
from timing light signals.

697
00:42:24,001 --> 00:42:26,541
Leave that drift alone,
and the map in your pocket

698
00:42:26,541 --> 00:42:29,721
would wander by around
ten kilometres every day.

699
00:42:29,821 --> 00:42:32,061
The system is built to correct it.

700
00:42:32,061 --> 00:42:35,241
Relativity is not a theory
sitting on a shelf.

701
00:42:35,281 --> 00:42:36,881
It is in your phone.

702
00:42:37,161 --> 00:42:40,661
In 1971 two physicists
bought airline seats,

703
00:42:40,661 --> 00:42:43,521
and flew atomic clocks
right around the world,

704
00:42:43,521 --> 00:42:46,261
once heading east and once heading west.

705
00:42:46,301 --> 00:42:51,021
When the clocks came home, they no longer
agreed with the clocks that stayed behind.

706
00:42:51,021 --> 00:42:56,101
They were off by billionths of a second,
by the amounts the theory predicted.

707
00:42:56,201 --> 00:42:59,421
So the question is not whether
moving clocks run slow.

708
00:42:59,421 --> 00:43:00,421
They do.

709
00:43:00,421 --> 00:43:02,361
The question is why.

710
00:43:02,821 --> 00:43:05,121
Here is the rule underneath it all.

711
00:43:05,621 --> 00:43:10,641
Light always passes you at the same
speed, however fast you are moving.

712
00:43:11,321 --> 00:43:16,681
Imagine a clock made of light: a
flash bouncing between two mirrors,

713
00:43:16,681 --> 00:43:18,861
one tick for every trip.

714
00:43:19,381 --> 00:43:23,181
Now put that clock on a fast
ship and watch it go past.

715
00:43:23,701 --> 00:43:26,981
From outside, the flash is not
going straight up and down any more.

716
00:43:27,541 --> 00:43:30,601
It is travelling on a long slanted path,

717
00:43:30,601 --> 00:43:33,461
because the mirrors moved
while it was in flight.

718
00:43:33,881 --> 00:43:37,361
The light cannot speed up to
cover the extra distance.

719
00:43:37,501 --> 00:43:39,761
So every tick takes longer.

720
00:43:40,201 --> 00:43:42,461
The moving clock runs slow,

721
00:43:42,581 --> 00:43:45,083
and so does everything moving with

722
00:43:45,083 --> 00:43:48,001
it: your heartbeat, your
thoughts, your ageing.

723
00:43:49,361 --> 00:43:53,821
At ten percent of light speed, the
slowing is about half a percent.

724
00:43:53,981 --> 00:43:59,201
At ninety percent, your time runs
at less than half the pace of home.

725
00:43:59,701 --> 00:44:03,041
At ninety-nine percent, seven to one.

726
00:44:03,501 --> 00:44:08,021
At ninety-nine point nine
nine percent, seventy to one.

727
00:44:08,621 --> 00:44:11,221
You do not need a spaceship to see it.

728
00:44:12,061 --> 00:44:16,761
Cosmic rays hit the top of the atmosphere
and make particles called muons.

729
00:44:17,121 --> 00:44:20,821
A muon lives only about
two millionths of a second.

730
00:44:21,181 --> 00:44:23,081
Even at nearly light speed,

731
00:44:23,081 --> 00:44:26,481
that is enough to cross only about
six hundred and sixty metres.

732
00:44:27,621 --> 00:44:31,081
Yet muons reach the
ground in great numbers.

733
00:44:31,621 --> 00:44:36,721
In 1963 physicists counted them
on a mountain and at sea level,

734
00:44:36,741 --> 00:44:41,241
and the numbers only made sense if
the muons' clocks were running slow.

735
00:44:41,961 --> 00:44:44,821
Several are passing through
you as you watch this.

736
00:44:44,821 --> 00:44:48,681
Now take that slow clock,
and put yourself inside it.

737
00:44:49,141 --> 00:44:51,101
You and your twin say goodbye.

738
00:44:51,361 --> 00:44:55,261
You board a ship that reaches
ninety-nine percent of light speed,

739
00:44:55,361 --> 00:45:00,461
fly to a star ten light-years away,
turn around and come straight back.

740
00:45:00,941 --> 00:45:04,501
On Earth, your twin waits
a little over twenty years.

741
00:45:04,601 --> 00:45:07,881
On board, about three years pass for you.

742
00:45:08,461 --> 00:45:10,821
You step off the ship three years older.

743
00:45:10,881 --> 00:45:13,481
Your twin is twenty years older.

744
00:45:13,821 --> 00:45:15,801
The trip did not damage you.

745
00:45:15,921 --> 00:45:18,381
It simply spent less of your time.

746
00:45:18,741 --> 00:45:21,481
From inside the ship, nothing feels slow.

747
00:45:21,581 --> 00:45:24,180
Your coffee cools at the normal rate.

748
00:45:24,501 --> 00:45:26,601
So what would you see out of the window?

749
00:45:27,041 --> 00:45:30,481
The stars ahead crowd together
and shift towards blue.

750
00:45:30,541 --> 00:45:33,861
The stars behind thin out and fade to red.

751
00:45:34,141 --> 00:45:37,501
And something else changes: the distance.

752
00:45:38,001 --> 00:45:41,281
Ahead of you, the gap
to that star shrinks.

753
00:45:41,401 --> 00:45:44,841
Ten light-years of space
becomes about one and a half.

754
00:45:45,021 --> 00:45:47,681
That is how you cross
it in so little of your

755
00:45:47,681 --> 00:45:51,441
own time without ever
passing the speed of light.

756
00:45:52,161 --> 00:45:53,301
But wait.

757
00:45:53,301 --> 00:45:56,521
From your seat, it was
Earth that was moving.

758
00:45:56,741 --> 00:45:59,021
Why isn't your twin the younger one?

759
00:45:59,601 --> 00:46:02,021
Because the two trips were not the same.

760
00:46:02,321 --> 00:46:04,621
Your twin never changed course.

761
00:46:04,761 --> 00:46:10,261
You fired your engines, stopped,
turned around and changed direction.

762
00:46:10,561 --> 00:46:14,941
In that turn you switched from
one frame of motion to another,

763
00:46:15,161 --> 00:46:17,301
and your twin never did.

764
00:46:17,921 --> 00:46:19,881
The paradox breaks there.

765
00:46:20,041 --> 00:46:23,321
The one who turns around
comes home younger.

766
00:46:24,221 --> 00:46:28,441
The clocks on the airliners settled
the same question with real hardware.

767
00:46:29,181 --> 00:46:30,701
So why haven't we done it?

768
00:46:30,821 --> 00:46:34,261
Because going fast costs
more than it seems.

769
00:46:34,521 --> 00:46:37,841
Take a single tonne, about
the mass of a small car.

770
00:46:38,141 --> 00:46:40,721
To push it to ninety
percent of light speed takes

771
00:46:40,721 --> 00:46:44,901
around a fifth of all the
energy humanity uses in a year.

772
00:46:45,241 --> 00:46:47,661
To push the same tonne
from ninety to ninety-nine

773
00:46:47,661 --> 00:46:51,181
percent takes nearly five times as much,

774
00:46:51,181 --> 00:46:56,101
close to a whole year of the
world's energy, for one small car.

775
00:46:56,401 --> 00:46:59,761
Each extra nine costs more than the last.

776
00:46:59,961 --> 00:47:03,961
Closer to the limit, the energy
needed grows without end.

777
00:47:04,181 --> 00:47:08,061
That is why nothing with mass
ever reaches light speed.

778
00:47:08,281 --> 00:47:10,161
It is not a wall you hit.

779
00:47:10,241 --> 00:47:13,261
It is a hill that keeps getting steeper.

780
00:47:13,565 --> 00:47:17,645
And at those speeds,
space itself turns hard.

781
00:47:17,885 --> 00:47:20,765
Every speck of interstellar dust becomes a

782
00:47:20,765 --> 00:47:23,645
tiny explosion against
the front of the ship.

783
00:47:23,985 --> 00:47:26,445
For scale, the fastest thing we have ever

784
00:47:26,445 --> 00:47:29,525
built is the probe that
skims past the Sun.

785
00:47:29,825 --> 00:47:34,085
It reaches about six hundredths
of one percent of light speed.

786
00:47:34,285 --> 00:47:38,125
Plans exist to push tiny chips,
lighter than a paperclip,

787
00:47:38,125 --> 00:47:40,685
to a fifth of light speed with lasers.

788
00:47:40,945 --> 00:47:43,425
Nothing human-sized is close.

789
00:47:43,925 --> 00:47:46,745
Here is the strange gift inside all this.

790
00:47:47,185 --> 00:47:51,485
A ship that could keep
accelerating at one Earth gravity,

791
00:47:51,665 --> 00:47:54,825
then turn and slow down
for the second half,

792
00:47:54,965 --> 00:47:57,825
could cross the whole Milky Way in a

793
00:47:57,825 --> 00:48:00,685
little over twenty years
of the crew's time.

794
00:48:01,305 --> 00:48:05,285
A human lifetime is enough
to reach almost anywhere.

795
00:48:05,605 --> 00:48:09,885
But on Earth, a hundred
thousand years would pass.

796
00:48:10,325 --> 00:48:13,845
Everyone you knew, and
everyone who remembered them,

797
00:48:13,865 --> 00:48:16,725
would be gone long before you arrived.

798
00:48:17,085 --> 00:48:19,725
You could not come back
to the home you left.

799
00:48:20,085 --> 00:48:23,205
Only to a home that had
kept going without you.

800
00:48:23,745 --> 00:48:27,045
That is the familiar danger
hidden in the title.

801
00:48:27,605 --> 00:48:29,005
It is not the speed.

802
00:48:29,325 --> 00:48:33,325
It is your own clock, drifting
away from everyone else's.

803
00:48:33,705 --> 00:48:36,025
You already live inside this.

804
00:48:36,205 --> 00:48:38,765
Your phone depends on it every day.

805
00:48:39,005 --> 00:48:42,185
A muon carries it through
your body as you sit here.

806
00:48:42,465 --> 00:48:45,205
The light from the screen
in front of you is

807
00:48:45,405 --> 00:48:48,685
travelling at the one speed
every clock agrees on.

808
00:48:49,685 --> 00:48:53,745
What if the speed limit of the
universe is not a wall at all,

809
00:48:53,785 --> 00:49:00,305
but a trade: the faster you go, the more
of everyone else's future you spend?

810
00:49:00,885 --> 00:49:04,625
The bigger question is where
you would choose to land,

811
00:49:04,725 --> 00:49:07,625
if you knew home would not be waiting.

812
00:49:07,784 --> 00:49:10,664
Even without a relativistic starship,

813
00:49:10,664 --> 00:49:13,824
light itself brings the deeper galaxy
to our eyes.

814
00:49:14,884 --> 00:49:18,764
Look 600 light-years out
to the shoulder of Orion,

815
00:49:18,764 --> 00:49:24,064
and you see a dying supergiant
764 times wider than the Sun—

816
00:49:24,904 --> 00:49:27,624
building the heavy elements
that made our world possible.

817
00:49:30,476 --> 00:49:34,256
Look at Orion this winter at
the orange star on its shoulder.

818
00:49:34,976 --> 00:49:36,096
That star is dying.

819
00:49:36,936 --> 00:49:38,876
So is Betelgeuse about to explode?

820
00:49:39,416 --> 00:49:41,036
And what would you see if it did?

821
00:49:42,156 --> 00:49:43,736
It is called Betelgeuse.

822
00:49:44,356 --> 00:49:46,276
One day it will blow itself apart,

823
00:49:46,676 --> 00:49:50,156
and for a few weeks it will
shine so brightly that you could

824
00:49:50,216 --> 00:49:51,796
see it in the daytime sky.

825
00:49:52,536 --> 00:49:54,916
Betelgeuse is a red supergiant.

826
00:49:55,436 --> 00:49:56,656
It is enormous.

827
00:49:57,256 --> 00:50:00,196
Put it where the Sun is, and
its surface would reach out past

828
00:50:00,216 --> 00:50:03,376
the orbit of Mars and out
beyond the asteroid belt.

829
00:50:04,256 --> 00:50:07,476
It is somewhere between five and
seven hundred light-years away.

830
00:50:08,116 --> 00:50:11,816
Even measuring that is hard
because the star is so big and so

831
00:50:11,936 --> 00:50:14,956
restless that its centre
is difficult to pin down,

832
00:50:15,736 --> 00:50:16,536
and it is young.

833
00:50:17,256 --> 00:50:20,976
Our Sun is four and a half billion
years old and halfway through

834
00:50:21,016 --> 00:50:21,556
its life.

835
00:50:22,436 --> 00:50:25,256
Betelgeuse is only about
ten million years old,

836
00:50:25,656 --> 00:50:27,636
and it is already near
the end of its life.

837
00:50:28,656 --> 00:50:29,596
Why so fast?

838
00:50:30,336 --> 00:50:31,336
Because it is heavy.

839
00:50:32,216 --> 00:50:35,916
With somewhere around fifteen
to twenty times the Sun's mass,

840
00:50:36,356 --> 00:50:38,396
it burns its fuel ferociously.

841
00:50:39,096 --> 00:50:42,736
It gives out around a hundred
thousand times as much light as the

842
00:50:42,796 --> 00:50:43,156
Sun.

843
00:50:44,016 --> 00:50:46,716
Big stars live fast and die young.

844
00:50:47,716 --> 00:50:48,856
You can find it yourself.

845
00:50:49,496 --> 00:50:52,876
On a winter evening, look for
the three stars of Orion's Belt,

846
00:50:53,256 --> 00:50:54,836
then go up and to the left.

847
00:50:55,576 --> 00:50:57,516
Its surface is cooler than the Sun's,

848
00:50:57,856 --> 00:51:00,276
and that is why it glows
orange instead of white.

849
00:51:01,176 --> 00:51:03,996
At the end of 2019,
something strange happened.

850
00:51:04,716 --> 00:51:07,736
Betelgeuse, normally one of
the brightest stars in the sky,

851
00:51:08,116 --> 00:51:08,996
began to fade.

852
00:51:09,796 --> 00:51:13,276
By February 2020, it had dropped
to about a third of its usual

853
00:51:13,296 --> 00:51:13,936
brightness.

854
00:51:14,476 --> 00:51:17,956
It was the faintest anyone had
measured it in more than fifty years.

855
00:51:18,756 --> 00:51:20,396
The question went all around the world.

856
00:51:20,956 --> 00:51:21,636
Was this it?

857
00:51:22,336 --> 00:51:24,176
Was Betelgeuse about to explode?

858
00:51:25,216 --> 00:51:25,776
It was not.

859
00:51:26,416 --> 00:51:27,836
Telescopes caught the answer.

860
00:51:28,336 --> 00:51:31,776
A huge bubble of gas had burst
out from the star's surface and

861
00:51:31,856 --> 00:51:32,596
drifted away.

862
00:51:33,236 --> 00:51:35,536
As it cooled, it turned
into a cloud of dust,

863
00:51:35,896 --> 00:51:38,356
and the dust blocked part
of the star from our view.

864
00:51:39,076 --> 00:51:41,956
A cool patch on the surface
made the dimming deeper.

865
00:51:42,836 --> 00:51:44,056
Why did it fool everyone?

866
00:51:44,656 --> 00:51:47,476
Because from Earth, all we
see is a point of light.

867
00:51:48,016 --> 00:51:51,876
When that point fades, the obvious guess
is that something terrible is happening

868
00:51:51,936 --> 00:51:52,556
inside.

869
00:51:53,376 --> 00:51:55,336
By the spring, Betelgeuse was back.

870
00:51:55,816 --> 00:51:57,916
But the scare taught
astronomers something.

871
00:51:58,536 --> 00:52:00,836
A dying giant does not fade quietly.

872
00:52:01,356 --> 00:52:03,776
It throws off pieces of itself as it goes.

873
00:52:04,796 --> 00:52:06,876
So how does a star like this die?

874
00:52:07,876 --> 00:52:09,956
All its life, a star is a balance.

875
00:52:10,396 --> 00:52:11,996
Gravity pulls everything in.

876
00:52:12,356 --> 00:52:15,676
The heat of nuclear fusion in
the core pushes everything out.

877
00:52:16,496 --> 00:52:20,316
For most of its life, Betelgeuse
fused hydrogen into helium.

878
00:52:20,996 --> 00:52:23,476
Now it is fusing helium into carbon.

879
00:52:24,356 --> 00:52:26,876
After that will come heavier
and heavier elements,

880
00:52:27,376 --> 00:52:29,816
each stage burning faster than the last.

881
00:52:30,556 --> 00:52:32,696
The final stages take only years,

882
00:52:33,096 --> 00:52:33,876
then days.

883
00:52:34,576 --> 00:52:36,216
The end of the line is iron.

884
00:52:36,936 --> 00:52:39,016
Fusing iron does not release energy.

885
00:52:39,316 --> 00:52:40,496
It costs energy.

886
00:52:41,136 --> 00:52:42,796
So when the core fills with iron,

887
00:52:43,136 --> 00:52:45,856
the fire goes out, and gravity wins.

888
00:52:46,736 --> 00:52:50,916
In less than a second, a core
heavier than the Sun collapses into

889
00:52:50,956 --> 00:52:52,596
a ball the size of a city.

890
00:52:53,396 --> 00:52:55,976
The outer layers fall in, bounce off it,

891
00:52:56,236 --> 00:52:58,116
and are blasted out into space.

892
00:52:58,816 --> 00:53:00,376
That is a supernova.

893
00:53:01,436 --> 00:53:03,536
What is left behind is a neutron star

894
00:53:04,016 --> 00:53:06,816
so dense that a teaspoon of
it would weigh as much as a

895
00:53:06,876 --> 00:53:07,416
mountain.

896
00:53:08,256 --> 00:53:10,296
If you saw our film on neutron stars,

897
00:53:10,656 --> 00:53:12,636
you know how strange that place is.

898
00:53:13,516 --> 00:53:16,676
Or if the core is heavy
enough, a black hole.

899
00:53:16,956 --> 00:53:19,856
So imagine the night Betelgeuse explodes.

900
00:53:20,776 --> 00:53:23,276
The first sign would not be light at all.

901
00:53:24,196 --> 00:53:28,096
It would be a flood of ghostly
particles called neutrinos

902
00:53:28,636 --> 00:53:30,596
streaming out of the collapsing core.

903
00:53:31,556 --> 00:53:33,316
They pass straight through the Earth.

904
00:53:33,796 --> 00:53:37,536
Detectors deep underground
would catch a sudden burst,

905
00:53:38,156 --> 00:53:41,996
and astronomers would get the alert
hours before the star brightened.

906
00:53:43,016 --> 00:53:44,616
Then the light arrives.

907
00:53:45,596 --> 00:53:50,116
Within days, Betelgeuse would
blaze as bright as the Moon at

908
00:53:50,236 --> 00:53:51,056
half phase.

909
00:53:51,816 --> 00:53:53,536
You could see it in broad daylight.

910
00:53:54,196 --> 00:53:54,716
At night,

911
00:53:55,236 --> 00:53:58,936
it would outshine everything
in the sky except the Moon.

912
00:53:59,876 --> 00:54:01,816
It would stay that bright for weeks,

913
00:54:02,416 --> 00:54:05,396
then fade slowly over months and years

914
00:54:05,916 --> 00:54:08,676
until the shoulder of
Orion was simply gone.

915
00:54:09,676 --> 00:54:11,796
The hunter would lose a shoulder,

916
00:54:12,636 --> 00:54:17,036
and every person who looked up
afterwards would see a different Orion

917
00:54:17,336 --> 00:54:18,356
from the one we know.

918
00:54:19,696 --> 00:54:20,616
Should anyone worry?

919
00:54:21,596 --> 00:54:21,896
No.

920
00:54:22,676 --> 00:54:26,736
A supernova only harms life
on Earth if it is within a few

921
00:54:26,776 --> 00:54:27,796
dozen light-years.

922
00:54:28,756 --> 00:54:30,856
Betelgeuse is far beyond that.

923
00:54:31,796 --> 00:54:32,736
We would get the show,

924
00:54:33,356 --> 00:54:34,356
not the danger.

925
00:54:35,456 --> 00:54:37,096
And here is a strange thought.

926
00:54:37,916 --> 00:54:41,476
The light from Betelgeuse
takes centuries to reach us.

927
00:54:42,576 --> 00:54:45,636
If it had exploded sometime
in the late Middle Ages,

928
00:54:46,176 --> 00:54:47,896
more than five hundred years ago,

929
00:54:48,756 --> 00:54:51,036
we would only be finding out about now.

930
00:54:51,816 --> 00:54:53,356
So when will it happen?

931
00:54:54,296 --> 00:54:58,996
The best estimates say Betelgeuse has
around a hundred thousand years left.

932
00:54:59,976 --> 00:55:02,656
That is the answer most
astronomers would give you.

933
00:55:03,516 --> 00:55:06,516
Not in our lifetimes, almost certainly.

934
00:55:08,256 --> 00:55:10,116
Why can't we just look and tell?

935
00:55:11,536 --> 00:55:14,736
Because the changes that
matter happen deep in the core,

936
00:55:15,256 --> 00:55:18,516
and the surface barely shows
them until the very end.

937
00:55:19,536 --> 00:55:24,156
From outside, a star with 100,000
years left and a star with a

938
00:55:24,216 --> 00:55:27,196
hundred years left can
look almost the same.

939
00:55:28,336 --> 00:55:30,416
And the star keeps surprising us.

940
00:55:31,736 --> 00:55:35,596
A few researchers have argued that
it could be much further along than

941
00:55:35,636 --> 00:55:36,116
we think

942
00:55:36,576 --> 00:55:38,476
and might go within centuries.

943
00:55:39,336 --> 00:55:40,896
Most do not agree.

944
00:55:42,236 --> 00:55:45,276
The honest answer is that
nobody can give you a date,

945
00:55:45,976 --> 00:55:49,796
and in twenty twenty-five,
astronomers reported something new:

946
00:55:50,516 --> 00:55:55,136
a faint glimmer right where a hidden
companion star was predicted to be.

947
00:55:55,856 --> 00:55:59,796
A small star circling Betelgeuse
every six years or so,

948
00:56:00,356 --> 00:56:01,636
lost in its glare.

949
00:56:02,856 --> 00:56:03,716
If it is real,

950
00:56:04,176 --> 00:56:08,516
it explains a slow pulse in
Betelgeuse's light that has puzzled

951
00:56:08,576 --> 00:56:10,016
astronomers for decades.

952
00:56:11,236 --> 00:56:13,196
The sighting is still tentative.

953
00:56:13,916 --> 00:56:18,236
The best chance to check comes in
late twenty twenty-seven when the two

954
00:56:18,376 --> 00:56:19,976
stars are furthest apart.

955
00:56:21,496 --> 00:56:23,896
We have watched stars explode before.

956
00:56:24,876 --> 00:56:26,476
In the year ten fifty-four,

957
00:56:26,936 --> 00:56:31,616
astronomers in China recorded a
new star so bright that it could be

958
00:56:31,696 --> 00:56:34,376
seen in daylight for
more than three weeks.

959
00:56:35,496 --> 00:56:37,796
Today, at that spot in the sky,

960
00:56:38,356 --> 00:56:39,656
there is the Crab Nebula,

961
00:56:40,196 --> 00:56:41,196
still expanding.

962
00:56:42,736 --> 00:56:45,196
So is Betelgeuse about to explode?

963
00:56:46,256 --> 00:56:46,936
Not tonight,

964
00:56:47,476 --> 00:56:49,816
and probably not for a very long time,

965
00:56:50,616 --> 00:56:51,316
but it will.

966
00:56:52,116 --> 00:56:52,956
And when it does,

967
00:56:53,416 --> 00:56:54,716
it will give something back.

968
00:56:55,816 --> 00:57:00,056
Much of the oxygen you breathe
and the calcium in your bones were

969
00:57:00,116 --> 00:57:02,876
made inside giant stars like this one

970
00:57:03,356 --> 00:57:05,936
and scattered across
space when they exploded.

971
00:57:07,016 --> 00:57:08,576
Betelgeuse will do the same,

972
00:57:09,196 --> 00:57:13,156
seeding the clouds that will
become new stars and new worlds.

973
00:57:14,736 --> 00:57:17,916
What if the most important
thing about a dying star

974
00:57:18,376 --> 00:57:19,636
is not how it ends,

975
00:57:20,156 --> 00:57:21,656
but what it leaves behind?

976
00:57:22,876 --> 00:57:25,256
The bigger question is closer to home.

977
00:57:26,316 --> 00:57:26,916
Long ago,

978
00:57:27,396 --> 00:57:30,136
giant stars near a cloud of gas and dust

979
00:57:30,636 --> 00:57:32,496
seeded it with new elements.

980
00:57:33,596 --> 00:57:36,596
That cloud became our Sun, our Earth,

981
00:57:36,876 --> 00:57:37,596
and us.

982
00:57:38,776 --> 00:57:42,696
We are made of stars that lived
and died before the Sun was

983
00:57:42,736 --> 00:57:43,156
born.

984
00:57:43,779 --> 00:57:48,219
From the calcium in our bones
forged inside dying supergiants,

985
00:57:48,479 --> 00:57:51,439
back across the light-years
to the red dust of Mars,

986
00:57:52,079 --> 00:57:56,119
the thick clouds of Venus, and
the spinning Earth beneath your feet—

987
00:57:56,939 --> 00:58:00,719
every world we study is part
of the same long story.

988
00:58:01,599 --> 00:58:04,439
Goodnight, and thank you
for watching with us.
